\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{When zeros fall between samples}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the centered symbol on a grid with an odd period, the zero near #{\pi} lies between two
samples, and \ref{fdip-0142} counts its degree in the off-grid contribution. For every grid size #{N\ge 5}, a range-two trigonometric symbol can vanish at a sample
and strictly between that sample and each of its two neighbors. Its affine interpolant
can miss both intervening zeros. When can a finite check exclude a zero of the interpolant between
samples, and what does the sign of a derivative at a vertex say
about the degree?}
